  "response": "Instrumental imaging opens the body of the picture. Infrared reflectography reveals underdrawing beneath the paint surface, so that the first laying-in of a head or a hand can be set beside the draughtsmanship recorded in securely attributed pictures. X-radiography reveals compositional changes and the weave structure of the support, registering at once the painter's revisions and the density pattern of the canvas or the panel on which the image was built.\n\nDendrochronology dates oak panels by matching their tree-ring sequences to reference chronologies assembled from timber of known date. Dendrochronology establishes the exact year in which a panel painting was executed. Yet a matched ring sequence terminates with the last ring surviving on the board and so fixes a date for the growth of the wood rather than for the picture, and a record of growth of that kind cannot be squared with a determination of the year in which a brush was put to the prepared ground. Phthalocyanine blue was first synthesized in the twentieth century, and such a date of origin shows that pigment analysis can identify materials that did not exist during the artist's lifetime.\n\nPigment identification bears directly on litigation. Titanium white was in common use in Dutch workshops of the 1650s, and its detection in the preparatory layers of a mid-century portrait therefore shows that every pigment present in the painting was available to painters of the seventeenth century. Either the paint film consists without exception of materials obtainable before 1700, or at least one pigment present in the painting was invented after the seventeenth century; one of those two accounts of the materials must hold of the picture, and they cannot hold together. Sotheby's sold a \"Portrait of a Gentleman\" attributed to Frans Hals in 2011. After that sale, expert witnesses instructed by the opposing parties gave conflicting opinions at trial. Even though those opinions proved irreconcilable, the High Court concluded that the painting was a modern forgery rather than a work by Frans Hals, the presence in the paint film of a pigment invented after the seventeenth century being the evidence that established that conclusion.\n\nConnoisseurship begins with the eye: the specialist compares a disputed picture's brushwork and handling with the artist's documented works, weighing the loading of the stroke, the economy of a highlight and the construction of a sleeve or a collar. Provenance research, by contrast, sets the painted surface aside altogether and traces a painting's chain of ownership back toward the artist's studio through household inventories, probate records, auction catalogues and dealers' stock books.",
